# Accepted historical gitleaks findings, one fingerprint per line.
#
# This file forgives one specific occurrence and nothing else: the same
# secret appearing in a new commit fails the scan again. That is what
# makes it the right place for a specific historical event, and the wrong
# place for content that recurs -- a documentation placeholder or a test
# fixture reappears in a new commit every time the text around it is
# edited, so it belongs in the [allowlist] regexes in .gitleaks.toml
# instead.
#
# The format is the Fingerprint line gitleaks prints for the finding:
#
#   commit:path:rule-id:line
#
# for example
#
#   69231b06a90e6e2155187016c4ca1f3715b63feb:divergulent/tests/test_rules.py:private-key:140
#
# Every entry needs a comment above it saying what the credential was and
# what was done about it. An undocumented entry is indistinguishable from
# a mistake.
#
# Never add an entry for a credential that still authorises something.
# History cannot be rewritten to unpublish anything from a public
# repository -- the objects survive in every fork and in GitHub's own
# reflog -- so an accepted finding is a claim that the credential has
# been dealt with where it was trusted, not that it has been tidied out
# of sight. Rotate first, then record it here.
#
# There are no accepted findings today.
